# genesis/orchestrator/manifests — orchestrator-rendered fleet manifests

Per-human, per-edgenode, and per-doorway k8s manifests rendered by the orchestrator. Conductor
config (the `webrtc_config` block and related tx5 settings) lives in the `humans/` and `edgenode/`
subtrees; network policies and namespaces live alongside. This manifest CLAIMS full responsibility
for the subtree (`covers: subtree`): the coverage walk terminates here, integrity by construction.

## Conductor-config invariant (edit-time advisory)

**`webrtc_config` is passed verbatim into tx5's serde-camelCase `WebRtcConfig`.** The one key that
bites is the ICE-server list: it MUST be `iceServers`, never `ice_servers`. An unknown field is
silently ignored by serde, so a snake_case spelling is not a warning — it is a total, silent loss
of every ICE server, and the fleet "works" until topology makes NAT traversal load-bearing (defect
class #1 of the five-defect convergence arc). This is the anti-pattern shape: *a config surface that
tolerates unknown keys turns a spelling into a silent capability loss.*

The `webrtc-ice-servers-camelcase` rule fires as an advisory (class stays `inject` because
`epr:validator-webrtc-ice-servers-key` is not registered in v1) when any `.yaml` write in this
subtree contains a webrtc/ICE key. It catches the dead-key reintroduction at edit time without
blocking legitimate manifest edits. The deterministic gate is
`scripts/ci/validate-conductor-config.sh`, which rejects the dead key by name at render/deploy.

See: `genesis/docs/content/elohim-protocol/history/2026-07-12-substrate-convergence-five-defect-arc.md`
